{"kind":"assert","text":"Request 7827ebac is not satisfied, and the way it became unsatisfied is worth recording: the load-flaky latency gate was removed rather than made robust, which is precisely the trade its conditions forbid.\n\nWHAT I FOUND AT CURRENT MAIN.\n\nThe stalled-resident assertion survives and was widened. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:1537 now bounds elapsed at orientationTimeout+2*time.Second, four seconds against the two-second production timeout, with a comment explaining the allowance covers the signed local fallback and scheduler overhead. The two failures the request recorded, 3.218s and 3.288s, would both pass under this bound. It is still an elapsed-wall-clock bound, so still load-sensitive in principle, but the observed failures are addressed and the reasoning is written down.\n\nThe warm-summary assertion is gone, and nothing replaced it. internal/statusview/view_test.go no longer contains any latency assertion; the depth-20000 work is now BenchmarkWarmDepth20000Summary. That benchmark asserts on size, len(body) \u003e= 64 KiB, not on latency. And it is never executed by a gate: `make test` runs `go test ./...` with no -bench, no CI workflow passes -bench, and I confirmed by running the package that no Benchmark line appears in the output.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING BOOKKEEPING. The request's conditions do not merely ask that these assertions stop failing under load. They ask that each either become load-insensitive, or state a budget over work done, or make a load-induced failure legible in the failure text — and separately that \"the warm-summary test continues to catch a real latency regression, demonstrated by slowing the summary path and watching it fail\". Converting the assertion to an ungated benchmark satisfies the first requirement by deleting the check, and fails the second outright. There is now no gate that would notice the warm summary path getting slower.\n\nI want to be careful about what I am not saying. I am not saying whoever made this change did the wrong thing in their own context; a p99 wall-clock bound that reddens at load average 25 is a genuine problem, and removing it is a defensible immediate response. I am saying the protection has not been restored since, and the open request that would have restored it is being read as substantially done. On a partial reading of the conditions I read it that way myself, and only found otherwise by fetching the full text — which is the same mistake I nearly made retiring 280c176e as satisfied.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not retiring this request, unlike eee72899 earlier today whose premise I disproved. Its premise is sound and half its work is genuinely outstanding: the latency regression detector needs rebuilding in a form that measures work done rather than wall time, or that reports contention distinguishably. I am leaving it open with this measurement attached so the next reader sees the state rather than the summary.\n\nThe remaining wall-clock exposure in the mcp bound is a judgement call I would not make alone: four seconds is generous on a developer machine and may still be tight on a contended hosted runner, and the honest fix is a budget over work actually done, which is a larger change than widening a constant."}